.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-network-set - Set one or more fields on an existing node-level network interface


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge network set <target-id> <iface> field=value [field=value ...] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Set one or more fields (e.g. mtu, vlan_filtering) on an EXISTING node-level
network interface via PVE's own two-phase stage/commit model: the change is
staged (PUT /nodes/{node}/network/{iface}), a guard proves every OTHER
interface on the node is unchanged (PVE's staged network config is
node-wide, not per-interface — an unrelated concurrent change would
otherwise be silently swept into the same commit), and only then committed
(PUT /nodes/{node}/network), which is the single call that actually
triggers ifupdown2's live reload.

.PP
Unlike "network bridge create/destroy", this command has no
--management-bridge flag: the guard here covers EVERY other interface on
the node, not one designated canary.

.PP
This command has NO --force override for the guard check, for the same
reason "network bridge create/destroy" doesn't: a mismatch means PVE staged
changes this command never asked for and knows nothing about — there is no
safe way to force past that.

.PP
If the change is made but its result cannot be re-read afterwards, a
one-line warning is printed on stderr; stdout is unchanged and the exit
status is still 0.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for set

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-network(1)\fP
